    "summary": "High‑energy Dark Star > Stomp > Morning Dew suite dominates",
    "blurb": "The show is built around an epic Dark Star → Stomp → Morning Dew marathon that showcases blistering improvisation. Jerry’s soaring leads and Phil’s thunderous bass drive the jam, while the rest of the set remains solid but less memorable.",
    "review": "November 13, 1972 at Soldiers' and Sailors' Memorial Hall finds the Dead in a rare, high‑energy mood, delivering a near‑45‑minute Dark Star → Stomp → Morning Dew suite that still thrills listeners. The band locks into an angular bass intro before launching into a deep, flowing jam; Jerry’s guitar wails with a melt‑face intensity while Phil’s thundering “stomp” anchors the improvisation. Keith’s keyboards shepherd the transition out of the dark vortex, and the rhythm section keeps everything tight despite the raw audience environment. The rest of Set 1 offers a respectable mix of folk‑rock staples—Cold Rain And Snow, China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider—but none reach the heights of the second‑set centerpiece.\n\nAmong the available tapes, the Owsley audience recording (gd1972-11-13.aud.owsley.gems.105812.flac16) captures the suite with vivid room ambience and excellent clarity, making it the definitive listen for this night. While other audience sources vary from good to poor, they all agree on the jam’s brilliance; the Owsley tape simply presents it without the muddiness that plagues many 1972 AUDs. Fans seeking the full emotional arc of this Kansas City performance should start with Owsley's gem and then explore the other tapes for alternative perspectives.",
